  "summary": "A casual summer kimono made of unlined cotton or hemp, typically worn at festivals and hot springs.",
  "description": "Yukata feature simple, bold patterns and lighter construction than formal kimono, with straight sleeves and a narrower obi. Their practical design evolved from bathrothes to become the most widely accessible traditional garment in Japan.",
  "culturalContext": "The yukata — Japan's unlined cotton summer kimono — grew from Heian bathing dress into festival-and-ryokan universal: fireworks-and-matsuri yukata with wind-bell and goldfish motifs, onsen-town rental culture, and naruko tenjin \"love charm\" traditions. Easy to wear (no full layers) and cheap, it democratized kimono aesthetics; contemporary designers (Sou Sou, Uniqlo collabs) keep the yukata as Japan's living summer fashion, and geta-and-fan pairings are the season's postcard image.",
